Subject: [PATCH] dsd: decimate the PCM output down to a rate the DAC accepts

The DSD to PCM conversion (dsd2pcm) always yields DSD rate / 8: 352.8 kHz
for DSD64, 705.6 kHz for DSD128. A DAC that stops at 192 kHz or less
refuses that rate and the track does not play, or plays at the wrong
pitch when something downstream (CamillaDSP) expects the real rate.

Add halving stages after dsd2pcm and pick the smallest number of halvings
that gives a rate in the device's supported list: 176.4 kHz for DSD64 and
DSD128 on a 192 kHz DAC. A device that accepts DSD rate / 8 keeps it
unchanged (no extra stage). Each stage is a Kaiser windowed sinc low pass
designed for the rate it receives: flat to 20 kHz (well under 0.001 dB) and
at least 100 dB down from the new Nyquist frequency up, so the cascade (up
to DSD512 down to 44.1 kHz) does not fold anything into the audio band.
The cost is about 52 million multiply-adds per second and channel for
DSD512 down to 44.1 kHz.

The 32 bit conversion is scaled by a margin in dB, -3 by default and never
above 0, taken from SWEETSPOT_DSD_MARGINE_DB: DSD masters may peak above
digital full scale ("over", up to +3 dB) and without margin they clip.

Source: ralph-irving/squeezelite 72e1fd8abfa9b2f8e9636f033247526920878718
Upstream: Not submitted (local change)

